<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet component="quiz">
    <title>Learning Target 1,2,3 Quiz - MTH 204</title>

    <page>
        <p>
            This quiz covers course learning target #1, #2, and #3.
            Read all <term>DIRECTIONS</term> carefully.
            Good luck!
        </p>

        <exercise>
            <title>Learning Target 3, First Attempt</title>

            <!-- Learning Target 3 (Attempt 1) -->
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can determine if and how a given vector can be written as
                    a linear combination of a given set of vectors and explain
                    what this means geometrically.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is the vector <m>\bvec = \threevec{-5}{ 1}{-4}</m> a
                        linear combination of <m>\uvec = \threevec{1}{1}{2}</m>
                        and
                        <m>\wvec =
                        \threevec{-1}{ 2}{ 1}</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find the weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p workspace="1in">
                        Is the vector <m>\cvec = \threevec{-5}{ 2}{-4}</m> a
                        linear combination of <m>\uvec = \threevec{1}{1}{2}</m>
                        and
                        <m>\wvec =
                        \threevec{-1}{ 2}{ 1}</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find the weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Learning Target 3 continues on back.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, and <m>\vvec_3</m>
                        be given by the figure below.
                        <!-- \scalebox{0.8}{\includegraphics{LT4-f-trans.eps}}  -->
                        Is <m>\vvec_3</m> a linear combination of <m>\vvec_1</m>
                        and <m>\vvec_2</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find the weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                        Write your response to the right of the figure.
                        <term>(Note</term>
                        <em> there is no scale on the axes, so you
                        can't determine coordinates for the various vectors;
                        just use the grid and figure appropriately.) </em>
                    </p>

                    <image source="images/LT3-grid1.png" width="50%"/>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <!-- Learning Target 2 (Attempt 2) -->
            <title>Learning Target 2, Second Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can use information about pivots to make conclusions about
                    the solutions of a system of linear equations, and explain
                    the underlying reasoning (using appropriate terminology)
                    regarding the existence and uniqueness of solutions and
                    consistency or inconsistency of a system.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For each of the following situations describing the pivots
                    in RREF of the matrix for a given linear system, determine,
                    with clear justification:
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                if the system is consistent or inconsistent, and
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                if the system is consistent, whether there are
                                infinitely many solutions or only one.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>

                <p>
                    For each situation, be sure to write a sentence to justify
                    your conclusions.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The augmented matrix for a linear system with 4
                        equations and 2 unknowns has 2 pivots: one in column 1,
                        and the other in column 2.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The augmented matrix for a linear system with 3
                        equations in 4 unknowns has 3 pivots, and there’s no
                        pivot in column 2 and no pivot in column 4.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The coefficient matrix of a linear system with 3
                        equations in 5 unknowns has 3 pivots.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Learning Target 1 (Attempt 3) -->
        <exercise>
            <title>Learning Target 1, Third Attempt</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    I can execute the next 2-3 steps of Gaussian elimination by
                    hand that are needed to work toward RREF (and recognize that
                    additional steps are needed to achieve RREF).
                </p>

                <p>
                    A given linear system has been represented by an augmented
                    matrix and Gaussian elimination has begun.
                    The current state of the process is shown below.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Execute the next <term>three</term> row operations that you
                    would perform to continue the process of Gaussian
                    elimination, clearly stating your steps by writing the
                    explicit row operations you did.
                    You should write at least three additional matrices to show
                    your work.

    
                    <md>
                        \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
          1 \amp 4 \amp 0  \amp 7 \\
          2 \amp 7 \amp 0 \amp 10 
       
          \end{array}\right]
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>